This is a very common question, and the core of the answer lies in **preventing circular references (cycles)**.

Here is a classic example to illustrate the problem:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

class Node {
public:
    std::shared_ptr<Node> next;
    ~Node() { std::cout << "Node destroyed\n"; }
};

int main() {
    auto a = std::make_shared<Node>();
    auto b = std::make_shared<Node>();

    a->next = b; // a owns b
    b->next = a; // b owns a

    return 0;
} // Memory leak! Neither a nor b is destroyed.
```

In this example, when `a` and `b` go out of scope, their reference counts drop to 1 (not 0), because they still point to each other. Therefore, their destructors are never called, resulting in a memory leak.

`std::weak_ptr` solves this by providing a reference to an object managed by `shared_ptr`, but **without increasing the reference count**. 

Here is the fixed version of the code using `weak_ptr`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

class Node {
public:
    std::weak_ptr<Node> next; // Changed to weak_ptr
    ~Node() { std::cout << "Node destroyed\n"; }
};

int main() {
    auto a = std::make_shared<Node>();
    auto b = std::make_shared<Node>();

    a->next = b;
    b->next = a;

    return 0;
} // No memory leak! Both a and b are destroyed.
```

By using `weak_ptr`, the reference counts of `a` and `b` are not increased when they point to each other. When `a` and `b` go out of scope, their reference counts drop to 0, and they are properly destroyed.

