# Differences between `std::map` and `std::unordered_map`

## Key Differences

| Feature | `std::map` | `std::unordered_map` |
|---|---|---|
| **Underlying Data Structure** | Red-Black Tree | Hash Table |
| **Ordering** | Keys are sorted | Keys are not sorted |
| **Search Time Complexity** | O(log n) | O(1) average, O(n) worst case |
| **Insertion Time Complexity** | O(log n) | O(1) average, O(n) worst case |
| **Deletion Time Complexity** | O(log n) | O(1) average, O(n) worst case |
| **Memory Overhead** | Less memory overhead | More memory overhead due to hash table buckets |
| **Requirement for Keys** | Keys must be comparable (`operator<`) | Keys must be hashable (`std::hash`) and comparable for equality (`operator==`) |

## Code Examples

### `std::map` Example

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> ages;

    // Insert elements
    ages["Alice"] = 30;
    ages["Bob"] = 25;
    ages["Charlie"] = 35;

    // Iterate - keys are sorted alphabetically
    std::cout << "std::map (sorted order):\n";
    for (const auto& pair : ages) {
        std::cout << pair.first << ": " << pair.second << "\n";
    }

    // Search
    if (ages.find("Bob") != ages.end()) {
        std::cout << "Found Bob!\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Output:**
```
std::map (sorted order):
Alice: 30
Bob: 25
Charlie: 35
Found Bob!
```

